{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"similar-snowdrop","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nWait, the log shows `OSError: WinError 10022` from `server_bind`, not an `AttributeError`. This implies `SO_EXCLUSIVEADDRUSE` *was* defined and `setsockopt` ran, but failed. If `setsockopt` failed, why did `super().server_bind()` then succeed, only for the *next* boot to fail? And why did MeridianServer bind fine on earlier boots?"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-22T08:22:36.037349700+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"similar-snowdrop","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nWait, `SO_EXCLUSIVEADDRUSE` might be the issue after all. It's supposed to be set before `bind`, which I'm doing. But it can fail if set after bind or on certain socket types, and `MeridianServer` uses `AF_INET` and `SOCK_STREAM` by default. I need to quickly check if Python's socket module actually exports `SO_EXCLUSIVEADDRUSE`."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-22T08:22:37.036377700+00:00"}}}}
