{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"similar-snowdrop","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nWait, the `port_owner` check failed to detect p1 because a `urllib` request timed out, possibly due to a busy server, even though `ThreadingHTTPServer` handles concurrency. Then, p1's own bind failed with `WinError 10022` on its very first launch, which is odd given that identical code worked for smoke tests moments earlier. The key difference might be `TIME_WAIT` socket states and the `SO_EXCLUSIVEADDRUSE` option, though a known issue with that option usually results in `WSAEINVAL`, not `10022`. Alternatively, `setsockopt` might have failed silently, allowing p1's bind to succeed, but then p2's boot would have been refused by `port_owner`, yet no `LAUNCH REFUSED` log exists."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-22T08:22:56.439360700+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"similar-snowdrop","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI've found the cause of the `WinError 10022` bind failure: `ThreadingHTTPServer` inherits `allow_reuse_address = 1`, and its `server_bind` method sets `SO_REUSEADDR` *after* `SO_EXCLUSIVEADDRUSE` is set. On Windows, these two socket options conflict, causing `bind` to fail with `EINVAL` (10022)."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-22T08:22:57.848839700+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"similar-snowdrop","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nBut wait — p1 bound OK on 9771 and on first 9764 boot... did it? The smoke test on 9771 definitely served. If reuse+exclusive always EINVAL'd, the smoke test would fail too. Unless allow_reuse_address... HTTPServer.server_bind calls socket.bind directly? Order: our server_bind → setsockopt(EXCLUSIVE) → super() → setsockopt(REUSEADDR) → bind. REUSEADDR set AFTER exclusive might invalidate exclusive, then bind might succeed but NOT exclusive! Why did 9771 work then? Maybe REUSEADDR clears the exclusive flag silently and bind works — meaning exclusive mode is OFF — dangerous!"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-22T08:22:59.839438200+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"similar-snowdrop","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nThe safest fix is to set `allow_reuse_address = False` on MeridianServer and also use `SO_EXCLUSIVEADDRUSE`. `SO_EXCLUSIVEADDRUSE` alone provides exclusive semantics, and Windows listeners are already exclusive-ish by default without `SO_REUSEADDR`. I'll set `allow_reuse_address = False` in the class."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-22T08:23:00.571561400+00:00"}}}}
